{"source_key":"sha256-db92dd9d54884ccb80de5ab4e2691d5a","record_index":271,"record_hash":"sha256:6d4a83ffb6cee786b93e7333eb4bb85cc623f8043072908264ba7cc2503b0cd6","timestamp":"2026-07-12T13:19:10.379Z","kind":"message","source_event_kind":"codex:response_item:message","role":"assistant","text":"The safe design is a two-release expand/contract migration:\n\n1. Ship a bridge release that eagerly finishes migration and teaches the oplog the new format.\n2. In the following release, remove `[default_target]` from the live `virtual_branches.toml` and make `ProjectMeta` the sole runtime source.\n3. Treat the bridge release—not an arbitrary older version—as the supported downgrade floor.\n\nA one-release removal cannot simultaneously provide reliable downgrade compatibility.\n\n## What exists today\n\n`ProjectMeta` contains:\n\n- `target_ref`\n- `target_commit_id`\n- `push_remote`\n\nThese live in the repository’s common local Git config under `gitbutler.project.*`, so linked worktrees share the same metadata. See [ref_metadata.rs]([REDACTED:path]) and [git_config.rs]([REDACTED:path]).\n\nFor ported repositories, Git config is already authoritative. However, writes still backfill the same values into legacy workspace metadata and ultimately `[default_target]` in `virtual_branches.toml`. That dual-write is centered in `ProjectMeta::persist()` and `Context::set_project_meta()` at [ref_metadata.rs]([REDACTED:path]) and [lib.rs]([REDACTED:path]).\n\nOne important correction to the premise: elapsed time does not guarantee every project was migrated. `Context::project_meta()` deliberately performs a pure legacy read when `portedMeta` is absent; porting only happens on a later metadata write. The test at [main.rs]([REDACTED:path]) explicitly verifies this. Dormant or read-only projects can therefore still have their only target in TOML.\n\n## The oplog problem\n\nThe oplog currently:\n\n- Reads `ProjectMeta` to find the target commit.\n- Stores the entire live `virtual_branches.toml` in the snapshot.\n- Restores that TOML.\n- Treats its legacy target as authoritative and copies it back into Git config.\n\nSee snapshot creation and restore in [oplog.rs]([REDACTED:path]).\n\nIf `[default_target]` simply disappears:\n\n- New snapshots no longer contain the target.\n- Undoing a base change cannot restore `ProjectMeta`.\n- The existing restore path can overwrite valid Git config with an empty target.\n- Older releases can partially mutate the repository before failing.\n\nThe oplog must therefore get an independent ProjectMeta representation.\n\n## Recommended snapshot format\n\nKeep snapshot version 3 and add an optional root blob:\n\n```text\nproject_meta.toml\n```\n\nFor example:\n\n```toml\ntargetRef = \"refs/remotes/origin/main\"\ntargetCommitId = \"0123456789abcdef...\"\npushRemote = \"origin\"\n```\n\nUse a tiny oplog-local DTO rather than making the core domain type serializable.\n\nNew snapshots should contain two representations with deliberately different purposes:\n\n- `project_meta.toml`: authoritative for current GitButler.\n- `virtual_branches.toml`: stack state plus a synthesized legacy `[default_target]`, only inside the immutable oplog snapshot.\n\nThe live `virtual_branches.toml` remains target-free. The legacy target exists only so a supported older binary can restore a new snapshot without clearing Git config.\n\nThe synthesized legacy value needs `remoteUrl`, which `ProjectMeta` intentionally does not store. Derive it from the configured remote, with an empty string as a best-effort compatibility fallback. Only synthesize it when both a target ref and non-null target commit exist; the old format cannot faithfully represent every partial ProjectMeta state.\n\nDo not remove `virtual_branches.toml` from the snapshot. Snapshot enumeration currently uses its presence to recognize oplog commits.\n\n### Do not bump the oplog version\n\n`get_workdir_tree()` treats exactly `Version(3)` as having the direct `worktree` subtree. Emitting version 4 would make current and downgraded binaries take legacy reconstruction paths and potentially lose or mishandle uncommitted work. See [oplog.rs]([REDACTED:path]) and [entry.rs]([REDACTED:path]).\n\nThe bridge release can change its reader to recognize `>= 3`, but should continue emitting version 3 for this additive change.\n\n## New restore behavior\n\nRestore should preflight and parse all required metadata before touching the worktree or refs.\n\nThen:\n\n1. If `project_meta.toml` exists, use it as authoritative.\n2. Otherwise, derive `ProjectMeta` from `[default_target]` in historical snapshots.\n3. Restore stacks and branch metadata from the snapshot TOML.\n4. Strip its compatibility `[default_target]` before writing the live TOML or importing it into the DB.\n5. Persist the selected ProjectMeta directly to Git config.\n6. Remove the call to `resync_project_meta_from_legacy()`.\n7. Restore refs, index, worktree, and invalidate caches as today.\n\nThis supports:\n\n| Scenario | Result |\n|---|---|\n| New binary restores old snapshot | ProjectMeta derived from legacy target |\n| New binary restores new snapshot | ProjectMeta read from the new blob |\n| Bridge binary restores new snapshot | Reads synthesized legacy target |\n| Pre-ProjectMeta binary opens target-free live TOML | Fundamentally unsupported |\n\nThe current target-undo test at [oplog.rs]([REDACTED:path]) is the natural starting point for the expanded coverage.\n\n## Downgrade policy\n\nRepository history gives three boundaries:\n\n- `0.20.0` and earlier only understand the live TOML target.\n- `0.20.1` introduced ProjectMeta/config-aware reads.\n- `0.20.4` contains later repairs and is a better floor for ordinary metadata behavior.\n\nHowever, I would not declare `0.20.4` a fully supported downgrade target after literal removal. It does not understand the new authoritative oplog blob, and after operating on a target-free live TOML it can create snapshots that its own restore path handles poorly.\n\nFor complete downgrade support—including undo/redo and snapshots—the supported floor should be the new bridge release.\n\nIf downgrade must include `0.20.0` or arbitrary historical versions, there are only two viable alternatives:\n\n- Keep a live write-only `[default_target]` mirror indefinitely.\n- Provide a newer-version “prepare for downgrade” command that rehydrates legacy TOML before installing the old application.\n\nThe first violates literal removal and preserves split-brain risk. The second cannot protect users who manually replace the application without running the converter. I do not recommend either unless the product requirement explicitly includes pre-ProjectMeta releases.\n\n## Implementation plan\n\n### Phase 1: bridge release\n\n1. Eagerly finish migration during project activation.\n\n   In [projects.rs]([REDACTED:path]), detect an unported project, resolve its legacy target, apply the existing repair rules, and persist it to Git config. Keep the live mirror during this release.\n\n2. Preserve `gitbutler.project.portedMeta=true`.\n\n   Even after the new version no longer needs it, the bridge and older ProjectMeta-aware releases require it to choose Git config over TOML.\n\n3. Extend oplog snapshot preparation.\n\n   In `gitbutler-oplog`:\n\n   - Resolve ProjectMeta once.\n   - Reuse the same value for target traversal and `project_meta.toml`.\n   - Produce an oplog-only legacy TOML copy with a synthesized target.\n   - Do not modify the live TOML to produce that copy.\n\n4. Implement dual-reader restore.\n\n   Prefer the new blob, fall back to old snapshot TOML, normalize the restored live file, and remove `resync_project_meta_from_legacy()`.\n\n5. Improve restore preflight.\n\n   Parse and validate both the project metadata and legacy branch state before checkout/ref/config mutation.\n\n6. Fix version feature detection.\n\n   Teach readers that snapshot versions `>= 3` carry `worktree`, but keep emitting version 3.\n\n7. Preserve cross-process notifications.\n\n   Today ProjectMeta writes indirectly touch `.git/gitbutler/REFRESH` through the TOML writer. Once dual-write stops, CLI and second-window target changes would become invisible to the desktop watcher. Move that best-effort sentinel write into the centralized successful ProjectMeta config-write path. The current writer is in [storage.rs]([REDACTED:path]).\n\n8. Add diagnostic logging.\n\n   Record when activation still needed a TOML fallback. This gives concrete evidence for when the final migration parser can be removed.\n\n### Phase 2: remove live fields\n\n1. Make `ProjectMeta::persist()` config-only—or collapse it into `persist_to_local_config()`.\n\n2. Simplify `Context::set_project_meta()` to:\n\n   - write Git config;\n   - touch the refresh sentinel when changed;\n   - invalidate the workspace cache.\n\n3. Replace production `set_default_target()` callers with ProjectMeta construction and persistence. The main callers are in [base.rs]([REDACTED:path]) and `but-action`.\n\n4. Remove `default_target` from the live `VirtualBranches` serialization shape.\n\n5. Stop mapping project fields through `VirtualBranchesTomlMetadata::workspace_from_data()` and `set_workspace()`. These mappings are currently in [mod.rs]([REDACTED:path]).\n\n6. Remove materialization-time `Workspace::set_project_meta()` calls where they only exist to backfill TOML. Notable sites include:\n\n   - `but-api/src/workspace.rs`\n   - `but-api/src/branch.rs`\n   - `but-transaction/src/lib.rs`\n   - `but-workspace/src/branch/move_branch.rs`\n   - `gitbutler-branch-actions/src/upstream_integration.rs`\n\n7. Retain a narrow input-only legacy parser.\n\n   It is still needed for:\n\n   - users upgrading directly from an old version without passing through the bridge;\n   - historical oplog snapshots.\n\n   It must never become a live source after successful migration.\n\n8. Normalize existing live files.\n\n   During the first writable activation after porting, rewrite `virtual_branches.toml` without `[default_target]`. Unknown legacy input should be accepted and then discarded.\n\n9. Keep the existing repair invariant.\n\n   Continue filling a missing `target_commit_id` from the target ref only on migration/recovery paths, and never overwrite an existing stable target commit.\n\n### Phase 3: later cleanup\n\nOnce the downgrade and old-snapshot support windows permit:\n\n- Remove the input-only live TOML fallback.\n- Simplify `ProjectMeta::resolve()` into direct config loading.\n- Remove the project fields and accessors from generic `ref_metadata::Workspace`.\n- Remove dormant `default_target_*` SQLite conversion code and columns only as part of an intentional database compatibility boundary.\n\nI would leave the SQLite columns nullable and unused for now. Dropping them provides little value and makes older binaries fail while opening the project database.\n\n## Required validation\n\nThe implementation is not complete without these scenarios:\n\n- An unported, read-only project is migrated safely on activation.\n- A direct upgrade from pre-ProjectMeta state preserves its target.\n- Live TOML no longer contains `[default_target]`.\n- Config retains all three ProjectMeta fields and `portedMeta=true`.\n- A target change made by another process triggers workspace refresh.\n- New snapshot → new restore round-trips all three fields.\n- Old snapshot → new restore uses the legacy fallback.\n- New snapshot’s compatibility TOML can be restored by the bridge release.\n- Restore leaves live TOML target-free.\n- Target-ref changes and target-commit-only updates are both undoable.\n- Malformed `project_meta.toml` fails before repository mutation.\n- Snapshot enumeration and worktree/untracked-file restoration remain version-3 compatible.\n- The bridge release can open and operate on a project after the removal release has run.\n- Database compatibility is verified against the bridge release.\n\nNo files were changed during this investigation. The minimality pass is why this plan uses one additive oplog blob, keeps version 3, preserves existing DB columns, and avoids a new migration subsystem.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:114-119|note=[prior ProjectMeta persistence and repair context]\nrollout_summaries/2026-07-09T10-10-26-Ab6G-pr14674_projectmeta_review_and_init_repair.md:34-48|note=[verified ProjectMeta init and migration repair behavior]\n<[REDACTED:path]>\n<rollout_ids>\n[REDACTED:entropy]\n<[REDACTED:path]>\n<[REDACTED:path]>","tool_name":null,"tool_input":null,"source_record":{"timestamp":"2026-07-12T13:19:10.379Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","role":"assistant","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"[REDACTED:entropy]"},"phase":"final_answer"}}}